we've covered using cloud monitoring in  your projects but what if you're  responsible for managing many projects  and you need to unify monitoring across  them and how can you control access to  monitoring data for many projects today  we'll look at cloud monitoring metric  scopes and learn how to use them to  monitor multiple cloud projects welcome  to engineering for reliability with  google cloud  let's start with a simple example how  can you make it easier to view  monitoring data from multiple projects  if i go to monitoring settings for my  project i can see that my current metric  scope only has a single project in it  the one i'm currently viewing i can add  others but what's actually happening  here let's have a look at metric scopes  and how they work  when you create a google cloud project  that project hosts a metric scope and  becomes the scoping project for that  scope it stores the alerts uptime checks  dashboards and monitoring groups that  you configure for the scope  for example if you create a staging  project and then access monitoring you  can see the metrics for the resources in  the staging project this happens for  every project you create each project  creates a metric scope for itself and  hosts monitoring configuration for  itself  but what if you want to centralize how  that data is stored and how it's  accessed you can add multiple projects  to an existing scope  now monitoring data for all projects in  that scope will be visible  this will let you do things like create  dashboards showing resources from all  the projects in the scope or alerting  policies that apply to resources in  multiple projects as long as they're in  the metric scope  note that the recommended approach for  production deployments is to create a  dedicated project to host monitoring  configuration data and use its metric  scope to set up monitoring for the  projects that have actual resources in  them this way should a project not be  necessary anymore and get deleted the  monitoring configuration for all the  other projects won't be impacted what  about access  while in this configuration users must  have appropriate iim permissions to  access monitoring data for example a  user would need to have the right role  in the monitoring project to be able to  see monitoring data for both projects  a user who only has access to the  staging project would not be able to see  any monitoring data for the production  project  now that you understand what metric  scopes are and what to use them for  let's have a look at how to set them up  in cloud monitoring  let's start in our staging project  we'll go to compute engine and see that  we have a single vm running  we can go to monitoring and see that  monitoring data for the vm is being  shown  for example on the infrastructure  summary dashboard let's now go to our  monitoring project  and confirm that there aren't any  compute instances running in it  we also don't have the out of the box  compute dashboards because we don't have  any resources running in this project  let's now add our staging project to be  monitored by this metric scope  we can do that in monitoring settings by  adding a project  selecting the project we want to add  and confirming that we want to use this  monitoring project as the scoping  project rather than creating a new scope  once this operation is complete  monitoring data from the staging project  will be visible in this metric scope  we can confirm that by going to the vm  instances dashboard for example  from here we can see more details about  the instance being monitored even though  it's running in another project  so that's how we can use the cloud  console to configure metric scopes and  collect monitoring data from multiple  projects  so that's how to configure metric scopes  in the ui but how can you figure out  where your project is being monitored or  where the monitoring data you can see in  your project is coming from  let's have a look  now that we have added our staging  project to our monitoring metrics scope  let's see how we can navigate between  the two we can see information about our  current metric scope here on the  monitoring overview page for the  monitoring project  if we click on that we can see that the  monitoring project is monitoring metrics  for resources running in the project  itself  and in our staging project we can go  there directly from here  in the staging project we can do the  same thing  and see that this project is monitoring  metrics for itself and that it is being  monitored by our monitoring project and  we can go back to that project simply by  clicking on that link  so that's how you can navigate between  metric scopes and monitored projects in  cloud monitoring  navigating metric scopes on the ui is  easy enough but how can you ensure that  for example new projects are added to  your production monitoring configuration  you can use the api to automate that  let's have a look  let's start in monitoring settings  and confirm that this particular metric  scope is only monitoring this project  which is the scoping project for this  metric scope  now let's go to our api  we'll use the create method for metric  scopes projects which takes two  arguments  first one is the parent which is the  metric scope that we want to add our  staging project to  the second is our project itself which  is a monitored project resource the name  field includes a reference to both the  staging project and the metric scope  we're adding this project to  let's execute this request  authenticate and confirm that the  operation is done  we can now go back to monitoring  settings for our monitoring project  refresh the page  and confirm that the staging project has  been added to this metric scope so  that's how you can use the cloud  monitoring api to manage how projects  are monitored by metric scopes  thanks for joining me today we covered  how metricscopes lets you configure  monitoring across projects and how to  manage them in the ui and using the api  i hope this helps you standardize and  automate your monitoring run more  reliable services and keep your users  happy thanks so much for watching don't  forget to like and subscribe to never  miss out on more engineering for  reliability with google cloud see you  soon
 

